First, the energy of the emitted photon is found from its wavelength:

\[
E_{\text{photon}} = \frac{hc}{\lambda}
\]

Using \(h = 6.626\times10^{-34}\ \text{J·s}\), \(c = 3.00\times10^8\ \text{m/s}\), and \(\lambda = 632.8\ \text{nm} = 6.328\times10^{-7}\ \text{m}\):

\[
E_{\text{photon}} = \frac{(6.626\times10^{-34})(3.00\times10^8)}{6.328\times10^{-7}}
\approx 3.14\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

This is the energy difference between the 3s and 2p levels. The 2p level energy is given as \(15.2\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}\). Therefore, the 3s level energy is:

\[
E_{3s} = E_{2p} + E_{\text{photon}}
\approx 15.2\times10^{-19} + 3.14\times10^{-19}
= 18.34\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

Assuming no loss in the helium-neon transition, the pumping energy required must raise the neon atom to the 3s level, so the required pumping energy is approximately:

\[
18.34\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

This matches option C, \(18.32\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}\), within rounding.

**Final answer:** (C) \(18.32\times10^{-19}\ \text{J}\)

